'' PRINT, measured from fbc -- see docs/internals/phase-6-print.md.
''
'' There is NO generic print entry point: every scalar type has its own, and the third
'' argument is a SEPARATOR MASK saying what follows THAT item: 0 nothing, 1 newline,
'' 2 tab.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/print.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 scope imbalance

'' A bare PRINT is a newline on its own.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintVoid(0, 1)

'' One entry point per type. A POINTER has none of its own and goes through
'' fb_PrintULongint -- measured, not inferred from it being pointer-sized.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintInt(0
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintDouble(0
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintULongint(0

'' A LITERAL has no descriptor, and fb_PrintString takes a descriptor rather than the
'' (pointer, length) pair fb_StrAssign takes. fbc borrows a temporary one; passing the
'' literal's bytes instead would have the runtime read a descriptor out of string data.
''
'' The length is the literal's EXACT length -- 3 for "lit" -- not the len+1 that
'' fb_StrAssign takes. The Z in the name is the difference.
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_StrAllocTempDescZEx(@@$str1, 3)

'' The three separator forms, which the AST could not previously tell apart:
''   print s; n  -> the ';' suppresses, so s carries 0 and n ends the line
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintString(0, @s, 0)
''   print s, n  -> the ',' tabs
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintString(0, @s, 2)
''   print s     -> nothing follows, so the newline
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintString(0, @s, 1)
'' EXPECT-NOT: <NO TERMINATOR>
'' GOLDEN: expected/print.out
